---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import MarginNote from '../components/MarginNote.astro';
import { formatLastReviewed } from '../lib/site-meta';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Security"
  view="Security"
  bluf="No login, no submitting forms, no database, no third-party tracking. On-page fill sheets do not post. Site posture in plain language."
  description="How jpcarpenter.com is protected — JP Carpenter. Static-first, strict browser rules, AI crawl policy."
>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Read" note="Skim first" />
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>How to read this page</h2>
      <p>
        Skim the labels. Open a row when you want the explanation, what it means, why it
        matters on a personal site, how it was done, and where the idea comes from. That is the
        same pattern I use when teaching: headline first, depth on demand.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technical evidence of how I verify controls lives in
        <a href="/labs">Labs</a>. This page is the posture brief.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="In force" note={`Reviewed ${formatLastReviewed()}`} state="verified" />
    <div class="prose security-teach">
      <h2>Controls in force</h2>
      <p>
        These are live choices for this website. Open any row for the lesson and citations.
      </p>

      <div class="expand-stack">
        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Shrink what can go wrong</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> the fewer moving parts a website has, the fewer
              ways it can be broken. This site is a set of finished pages, like printed sheets,
              not a living application that talks to a database when you visit.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> there is no password page for an attacker to
              guess, and no admin panel hanging off the public site.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> pages are finished files on the server, not an app that
              rebuilds itself from a database on every visit. Fewer moving parts first; fancy
              gadgets only if they earn their keep.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                OWASP thinking on reducing unnecessary surface:
                <a href="https://owasp.org/www-project-web-security-testing-guide/">Web Security Testing Guide</a>
                (configuration / deployment testing).
              </li>
              <li>
                Same idea in practice: prefer a simple static site over stacking defenses on a
                complicated one.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Tell the browser what scripts are allowed</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> a
              <abbr title="Content-Security-Policy, browser rules for what a page may load">Content-Security-Policy (CSP)</abbr>
              is a packing list the browser must follow. This site's list says: only load scripts
              and styles from this same website, not from random other places, and do not run
              leftover "inline" script tags that often show up in attacks.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> even a simple page can be damaged if someone
              finds a way to inject script. A strict CSP makes that much harder. We also ask
              supporting browsers for
              <abbr title="Trusted Types, browser rules that block unsafe script sinks in the page">Trusted Types</abbr>,
              an extra guard against a common class of page-tampering bugs.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> every script and stylesheet ships as its own file from this
              website. The policy uses <code>script-src 'self'</code> with no
              <code>'unsafe-inline'</code>, plus <code>form-action 'none'</code> (fill sheets
              on /resources do not submit) and <code>frame-ancestors 'none'</code> (do not embed this site in someone
              else's frame).
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Content_Security_Policy_Cheat_Sheet.html">OWASP Content Security Policy Cheat Sheet</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Guides/CSP">MDN: Content Security Policy</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Trusted_Types_API">MDN: Trusted Types API</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Keep the site locked to HTTPS</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> visitors should always use the secure connection
              (the lock in the address bar).
              <abbr title="HTTP Strict Transport Security, tells browsers to use HTTPS only">HSTS</abbr>
              is a note to the browser: "for a long time, only use HTTPS for this name, and for
              its subdomains."
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> without HSTS, a first visit or a bad link can
              still try plain HTTP. HSTS closes that gap after the browser has seen the policy.
              The <code>preload</code> flag is consent to join the browser vendors' shared list.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> HTTPS is required; HTTP redirects to HTTPS. The site sends
              <code>Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload</code>
              from one nginx header snippet (HTML and hashed <code>/_astro/</code> assets get the
              same policy). The unused <code>www</code> DNS record was removed so the name list
              matches the certificate. The domain is submitted to the shared preload list
              (hstspreload.org status: <code>pending</code> — browsers pick it up after Chromium
              ships the entry; that lag is normal).
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Strict_Transport_Security_Cheat_Sheet">OWASP HTTP Strict Transport Security Cheat Sheet</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Strict-Transport-Security">MDN: Strict-Transport-Security</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://hstspreload.org/">hstspreload.org</a>, submission rules and caveats
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Lock who may issue the site's certificates (CAA)</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong>
              <abbr title="Certification Authority Authorization, DNS records that name which certificate companies may issue for your domain">CAA</abbr>
              records in DNS say which certificate companies are allowed to issue a lock
              certificate for this domain.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> without CAA, any public CA can issue a cert
              for the name if someone tricks the issuance process. CAA makes "wrong issuer"
              fail closed at the CA, not after the fact.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> DNS CAA on the apex names Let's Encrypt
              (<code>issue "letsencrypt.org"</code>) and routes issuance reports to
              <code>mailto:jp@jpcarpenter.com</code>. Verify with
              <code>dig CAA jpcarpenter.com</code>. If the site later moves to Cloudflare Pages,
              replace these with Cloudflare's CA set before cutover — a stale Let's Encrypt-only
              CAA will block their issuance.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8659">RFC 8659, DNS Certification Authority Authorization (CAA) Resource Record</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://letsencrypt.org/docs/caa/">Let's Encrypt: CAA</a>, how issuers
                check these records
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Stop framing tricks and casual browser features</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> other sites should not wrap this page in a
              picture-frame and trick someone into clicking the wrong thing. This site also
              turns off browser features a portfolio never needs (camera, microphone, location,
              payments, and similar).
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> clickjacking and cross-window tricks are old,
              but still cheap for attackers. Turning off unused device APIs is free hygiene,
              there is no legitimate reason for this site to ask for your camera.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> framing is denied; content-type sniffing is disabled;
              cross-origin opener and resource policies keep windows and assets on a short leash;
              Permissions-Policy is a long deny list.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Headers_Cheat_Sheet.html">OWASP HTTP Headers Cheat Sheet</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy">MDN: Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Permissions-Policy">MDN: Permissions-Policy</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                OWASP ASVS (Application Security Verification Standard), HTTP security header
                expectations, scaled to a personal static site (Level 1 thinking, not a bank).
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Opt out of AI training, stay findable in AI search</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> many AI companies now use <em>different</em> robots
              for "copy this into the training pile" versus "index this so people can find it in
              AI search." This site says no to the training robots and yes to ordinary search and
              AI search/citation robots.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> blocking everything hides the work from people
              who search. Allowing everything feeds training without a separate decision. The
              split is the thoughtful middle: stay discoverable, decline silent harvest.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> rules live in <a href="/robots.txt">/robots.txt</a>. That file
              is a request polite operators honor, not a hard lock against rude scrapers (rate
              limits on the origin handle the rest). Training bots such as <code>GPTBot</code> and
              <code>ClaudeBot</code> are Disallow. Live citation / user-fetch tokens such as
              <code>OAI-SearchBot</code>, <code>ChatGPT-User</code>, <code>Claude-SearchBot</code>,
              and <code>Claude-User</code> are Allow so a recruiter asking an assistant about this
              site can still get a live fetch.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/bots">OpenAI: Overview of crawlers</a>
                (GPTBot vs OAI-SearchBot vs ChatGPT-User)
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/8896518-does-anthropic-crawl-data-from-the-web-and-how-can-site-owners-block-the-crawler">Anthropic: Claude crawlers and robots.txt</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                Google's <code>Google-Extended</code> control, training/Gemini opt-out without
                turning off normal Google Search (documented in Google's AI / robots guidance)
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Markdown identity card (same facts, no cloaking)</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> there is a plain Markdown card at
              <a href="/about.md">/about.md</a> with the same biography, credentials, and contact
              facts as the HTML About page. Browsers keep the HTML page. Agents that prefer
              <code>text/markdown</code> can take the card.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> this is how you "only show them what you want"
              without lying. Same facts, denser format. Different facts for bots would be cloaking
              and would burn trust with search engines.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> <code>/about.md</code> ships as <code>text/markdown</code>.
              On the origin, nginx Accept negotiation serves that card for
              <code>/about</code> when the client asks for Markdown, and always sends
              <code>Vary: Accept</code>. Browsers still get HTML.
              <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> remains the denser whole-site summary.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Limits</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>Anyone can fetch <code>/about.md</code> directly. It is not a secret channel.</li>
              <li>Without edge bot verification, a scraper can still take the HTML.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Identity stitching (sameAs)</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> the About page marks up a Person with LinkedIn and
              GitHub links as <code>sameAs</code>, so crawlers can treat the site, LinkedIn, and
              GitHub as one person instead of three strangers.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> recruiter tools lean on LinkedIn and GitHub.
              The personal site is the identity hub. <code>sameAs</code> is the cheap stitch.
              Microdata is used instead of JSON-LD because this site's CSP forbids inline script
              and a static host cannot rotate nonces.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> schema.org Person microdata on <a href="/about">/about</a>,
              mirrored in <a href="/about.md">/about.md</a>.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Terms against training scrapes</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> <a href="/terms.txt">/terms.txt</a> says automated
              bulk collection for model training is not allowed without written consent, while
              ordinary reading, linking, hiring review, and robots-respecting search/citation stay
              welcome.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> a clear permission statement gives a hook for
              takedown or dispute letters. It does not stop a rude bot by itself.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> public terms file plus the robots split above. Weekly access-log
              review for AI user-agents remains an ops habit, not a page feature.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Same facts for machines, denser format (llms.txt)</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> there is a plain-text summary at
              <a href="/llms.txt">/llms.txt</a> written for automated readers. It packs the same
              biography, work claims, and lab findings into a structure machines parse easily.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> giving bots a denser copy of the truth is not
              the same as showing them a <em>different</em> story. Cloaking (one page for humans,
              another for bots) is dishonest and gets sites penalized. One truth, two formats.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> <code>llms.txt</code> is a public file. It is not in the
              primary navigation so humans are not pushed through it. Anyone who opens the URL
              sees the same text a bot would. Evidence that <code>llms.txt</code> moves rankings
              on personal sites is weak; keep it as housekeeping, not a growth lever.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Emerging <code>llms.txt</code> convention for structured site summaries for LLM /
                agent readers (paired here with honest robots policy, not as a substitute for it).
              </li>
              <li>This site's rule: no cloaking, documented on this page and in <code>llms.txt</code>.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Tell researchers how to report a problem</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> if someone finds a real issue with this website,
              they should know who to email, without hunting through contact pages.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> disclosure channels are basic operational
              hygiene. On a small static site the risk surface is small, but the courtesy still
              matters.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong>
              <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a> points to
              <a href="mailto:jp@jpcarpenter.com">jp@jpcarpenter.com</a> and links back here.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9116">RFC 9116, A File Format to Aid in Security Vulnerability Disclosure (security.txt)</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Quiet tripwire for scanners</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> there is an unlinked decoy file on the site. Normal
              readers never see it. If something requests it, that shows up in server logs,
              useful as an early "someone is poking around" signal.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> it does not stop an attack by itself. It helps
              detection. The address of the decoy is not published here on purpose, advertising
              a tripwire teaches scanners where to step.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> a small unused file ships with the site; server access logs
              are checked for requests to it.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Common defensive pattern: unlinked "canary" or honey paths as an early warning
                when something probes beyond normal browsing, without changing what real
                visitors see.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Origin nginx hygiene</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> the public site is static files behind nginx on a
              Njalla VPS. The server should not advertise its exact version, should send one
              consistent security-header set, and should not leave old TLS generations enabled
              "just in case."
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> version banners help scanners triage targets.
              Split header configs create gaps (for example assets without HSTS). Old TLS is free
              attack surface on a site that never needs it.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> <code>server_tokens off</code> (responses say
              <code>Server: nginx</code> without a version). One header snippet covers HTML and
              <code>/_astro/</code> assets: 1-year HSTS with <code>includeSubDomains; preload</code>,
              <code>X-Frame-Options: DENY</code>, and the rest of the posture headers. TLS 1.0 and
              1.1 are disabled in the TLS config. Ghost stays off the public origin.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_core_module.html#server_tokens">nginx server_tokens</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Headers_Cheat_Sheet.html">OWASP HTTP Headers Cheat Sheet</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Slow down rude scrapers (gentle rate limits)</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> <code>robots.txt</code> is a polite sign on the
              door. Some crawlers ignore signs. Soft rate and connection limits on the web server
              slow machines that hammer the site, without putting a puzzle in front of every
              human visitor.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> a static portfolio does not need a bot wall.
              It does need a ceiling so one IP cannot soak the origin with thousands of requests
              per second. Limits are set high enough that a normal page load (HTML + many assets)
              never notices.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>How:</strong> nginx <code>limit_req</code> at <code>20r/s</code> per IP with
              <code>burst=80 nodelay</code>, plus <code>limit_conn</code> at 40 concurrent
              connections per IP. Over-limit responses are <code>429</code>. Verified: ordinary
              browsing and a 30-request burst return normally.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_limit_req_module.html">nginx limit_req module</a>
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://nginx.org/en/docs/http/ngx_http_limit_conn_module.html">nginx limit_conn module</a>
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Why there is no CAPTCHA here</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              <strong>In plain terms:</strong> those "prove you are human" puzzles are useful on
              login forms and signup walls. This site has neither. A CAPTCHA on every visit would
              mostly annoy people, and most scrapers never run the puzzle anyway; they just
              download the page.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Why it matters here:</strong> adding a third-party CAPTCHA would require
              outside JavaScript and punch holes in the strict script policy above. That trades a
              real control for theater. If contact spam ever becomes a real problem, the honest
              next step is a first-party form with a honeypot (and a deliberate policy review),
              not a badge that weakens CSP.
            </p>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Judgment call: do not put CAPTCHA on first contact without evidence of abuse,
                especially when it would force weaker browser script rules.
              </li>
              <li>CSP integrity citations in "Tell the browser what scripts are allowed" above.</li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band sheet">
    <MarginNote stamp="Refuse" note="On purpose" />
    <div class="prose security-teach">
      <h2>What we refuse</h2>
      <div class="expand-stack">
        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Security theater that hurts people or weakens real controls</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <ul>
              <li>Sitewide CAPTCHA / bot walls on a site with no login (see row above).</li>
              <li>Fake "military-grade" badges or seals with nothing to verify.</li>
              <li>Client-only "encryption" of public HTML, visitors already receive the page.</li>
              <li>
                Heavy challenge pages for every human on a low-traffic personal site (including
                self-hosted proof-of-work gates) unless availability is actually under attack.
              </li>
              <li>Third-party scripts "for convenience" that force permanent CSP exceptions.</li>
            </ul>
            <p><strong>Sources</strong></p>
            <ul>
              <li>
                Prefer real controls over badges and puzzles that weaken browser protections;
                same judgment as the CAPTCHA row above.
              </li>
              <li>
                <a href="https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/HTTP_Headers_Cheat_Sheet.html">OWASP HTTP Headers Cheat Sheet</a>
               , configure headers that matter; skip decoration.
              </li>
            </ul>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="expand-item">
          <summary class="expand-term">Check it yourself</summary>
          <div class="expand-body">
            <p>
              You do not have to take this page's word for it. From a terminal, or using your
              browser's developer tools on any page, look for the security headers on the
              response:
            </p>
            <pre class="security-curl"><code>curl -sI https://jpcarpenter.com | grep -Ei 'strict-transport|content-security|x-frame|permissions'</code></pre>
            <p>
              Also open <a href="/robots.txt">/robots.txt</a> and
              <a href="/.well-known/security.txt">/.well-known/security.txt</a>.
            </p>
            <p>
              For how I demonstrate verification craft (not just configuration claims), see
              <a href="/labs">Labs</a>.
            </p>
            <p><em>Last reviewed: {formatLastReviewed()}.</em></p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .security-teach {
    max-width: 40rem;
  }

  .security-teach .expand-stack {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .security-curl {
    margin: 1rem 0 0;
    padding: 0.85rem 1rem;
    overflow-x: auto;
    font-family: var(--font-mono, "Space Mono", ui-monospace, monospace);
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    line-height: 1.45;
    background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink, #1c1a16) 4%, transparent);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--sage, #64705b);
  }
</style>
